description:
pkix-chain/src/lib.rs:1609-1612 uses a hand-rolled const block to assert `Error: Send + Sync`:

```rust
const _: fn() = || {
    fn _assert_send_sync<T: Send + Sync>() {}
    _assert_send_sync::<Error>();
};
```

The workspace's review-rusty prompt explicitly notes that `static_assertions::assert_impl_all!` used internally is **encouraged**:

> `static_assertions` (`assert_eq_size!`, `assert_impl_all!`) used internally: encouraged. Compile-time invariants that would otherwise live only in comments.

The idiomatic equivalent is one line:

```rust
static_assertions::assert_impl_all!(Error: Send, Sync);
```

This:
- Renders the intent in the line itself (no `_assert_send_sync` helper to read past)
- Produces clearer compile errors when the assertion fails (the macro names the type and the bound, the hand-rolled version produces a generic "the trait bound is not satisfied" message far from the call site)
- Is the documented pattern other workspace crates already use (PKIX-2l0v.2 stance)

What goes wrong if it stays: every reader has to parse the closure/inner-fn dance to confirm it does what `assert_impl_all!` does in one well-known idiom. The pattern is also harder to grep for — a new contributor looking for "how do I assert Send+Sync in this workspace?" will not find this incantation by searching for the obvious keyword. Workspace dep cost: `static_assertions = "1"` is ~5kB of compile-time-only macros.
